This chapter specifies what `morloc make` produces and how the result runs. A
compiled program is a manifest describing its commands, one pool per language
holding the generated code, and a launcher. At run time the nexus reads the
manifest, starts the pools, and sends the first call; the pools then call one
another directly. The chapter defines the process lifecycle and worker
model, the protocol the processes speak, the shared memory and binary formats
that data travels in, streams, how errors propagate, and the data flow
through the CLI's collection, parsing and rendering stages.

